  "title": "'kept growing' is reported for ordinary discovery of an existing tree",
  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An operator investigates failed cleanup of processes that already existed when cleanup began. If discovery reveals them across multiple scans, the error incorrectly suggests continued process creation and can send the investigation toward the wrong cause.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "REVIEW_CHECKOUT=\"$PWD\" bun test \"$TMPDIR/limitless-561-adjudication.test.ts\"",
    "output": "F2 {\"bornBeforeCleanup\":true,\"scans\":3,\"error\":\"Error: Invocation termination could not be confirmed: Marked processes still alive: 2: <owned-pid> (sleep), <owned-pid> (sleep); set kept growing\"}\n(pass) F2: discovery of two already-existing sleepers reports growth\n\n2 pass\n0 fail"
   },
   "evidence": "The temporary probe started two owned sleepers and verified their native birth timestamps preceded cleanup. Injected snapshots exposed one sleeper initially and both thereafter; signals to those sleepers were suppressed and an injected clock reached the deadline. HEAD executes `if (observed) growing = true` whenever another process is claimed after a populated scan, then reports `set kept growing`. It never compares birth time with cleanup start, so discovery order is mistaken for continued process creation. Base had no growth diagnostic. The defect affects the requested distinction between a forking tree and slow cleanup, rather than the cleanup verdict itself."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "When cleanup gives up on a process tree that already existed before cleanup started, but found it over two scans instead of one, the error says the set 'kept growing'. An operator then blames a forking agent when the real cause was a slow machine, which is exactly the distinction the request asked the message to make.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun test test/hardening.test.ts -t \"surviving descendants block cleanup\"",
    "output": "2 pass, 0 fail. The growing=true case spawns two /bin/sleep 30 processes before cleanup (neither forks), shows one in the first scan and both afterwards, and asserts `expect(message).toContain(growing ? \"set kept growing\" : \"set remained stable\")`. So head's own test shows that discovery order alone produces 'kept growing' for processes that never forked."
   },
   "evidence": "Head, src/util/proc.ts:245-259: `let growing = false, observed = false; ... if (birth !== null) { if (observed) growing = true; claimed.set(...) } ... observed ||= claimed.size > 0;`. Growth is inferred from the order processes were discovered, not from whether they were born after cleanup started, so a pre-existing tree that a scan reveals late counts as growing."
  }
 }
